  "name": "Question 18",
  "problem": "In **ascending order,** the $x$-coordinates of the points where the hyperbola $14{x}^{2}-5{y}^{2}=64$ intersects the line $y=\\sqrt{2}x$ are\n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}.",
  "worked_solution": "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $y=\\sqrt{2}x,$ we first replace $y$ with $\\sqrt{2}x$ in the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14{x}^{2}-5{y}^{2} & =64 \\\\ 14{x}^{2}-5(\\sqrt{2}x)^{2} & =64 \\\\ 14{x}^{2}-5(2{x}^{2}) & =64 \\\\ 14{x}^{2}-10{x}^{2} & =64 \\\\ 4{x}^{2} & =64 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the $x$-coordinates of the points of intersection are\n$x=-4$ and $x=4.$",
